THOUGHT: `getCurrentKeyFrame()` is annotated `@Nullable` and simply returns `currentFrame`, so NullAway rightfully complains about dereferencing it in `update()`. The safest minimal fix is to cache the result in a local variable, check for null, and early-return if it’s null before using it. This preserves behavior (if there is no current frame, the animation effectively can’t advance) and avoids touching other logic. After editing the file, I’ll run the Gradle compile to ensure the NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/litiengine && sed -i '315,323s/if (!this.isPlaying()
-        || Game.time().since(this.lastFrameUpdate) < this.getCurrentKeyFrame().getDuration()) {
-      return;
-    }/    final KeyFrame currentKeyFrame = this.getCurrentKeyFrame();
    if (!this.isPlaying() || currentKeyFrame == null
        || Game.time().since(this.lastFrameUpdate) < currentKeyFrame.getDuration()) {
      return;
    }/' src/de/gurkenlabs/litiengine/graphics/animation/Animation.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -n "DEREFERENCE_NULLABLE" -n new_build_output.log | head && tail -n 20 new_build_output.log```

